<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taiwan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Taiwan is a large island off the south-eastern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> established a colony there in the seventeenth century but lost it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koxinga">Koxinga</a>'s forces in 1662, and the Qing took the island from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-family">Zheng family</a> in 1683. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sino-japanese-war">First Sino-Japanese War</a> of 1894–1895 it became a Japanese colony, supplying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Champa grew out of the Sa Huỳnh culture, an Austronesian seafaring people settled on the coast of present-day central Vietnam between about 1000 BCE and 200 CE, who traded across the South China Sea with the Philippine archipelago and <b>Taiwan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/champa">Champa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Dutch overseas expansion faltered after the loss of <b>Taiwan</b> to Koxinga's forces in 1662 and of New Netherland and New Amsterdam to England in 1664, and the Fourth Anglo-Dutch War of 1780–1784 left both the VOC and the GWC mortally wounded.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Colonial acquisitions structured the empire's economic geography hierarchically: <b>Taiwan</b> supplied sugar and rice; Korea provided rice, labour, and later industrial output; Manchukuo after 1932 supplied coal, iron, and strategic minerals essential for military production.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Chinese People’s Political Consultative Conference adopted a Common Program in September, and on 1 October 1949 Mao proclaimed the People’s Republic from the Gate of Heavenly Peace in Beijing, which again became the capital, while the Nationalist government withdrew to <b>Taiwan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…of the Tarim Basin in Eastern Turkestan, campaigns in Burma, Vietnam and Nepal, the suppression of rebellion on <b>Taiwan</b> and the two Jinchuan wars in Sichuan, represented the height of Qing military power and extended the empire to its greatest extent of some 13 to 14.7 million square…</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>On <b>Taiwan</b> after 1949, land reform culminating in the Land-to-the-Tiller programme of 1953, American aid and export-led manufacturing produced rapid growth, and by the 1990s the island was a leading producer of electronics and semiconductors.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-china">Republic of China · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
